1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:24,430
Now that man has learned to package and control a vastly greater force than chemical explosives,

2
00:00:24,430 --> 00:00:31,430
his thinking has turned to methods which will make possible the effective use of this new power within industry.

3
00:00:32,430 --> 00:00:34,430
The potential applications?

4
00:00:36,430 --> 00:00:37,430
Consider just one.

5
00:00:38,430 --> 00:00:42,430
The development of a harbor in the hard rock of a remote coastal area.

6
00:00:46,430 --> 00:00:49,430
Assume a channel approximately 400 meters wide,

7
00:00:50,430 --> 00:00:53,430
1500 meters long, and at least 20 meters deep.

8
00:00:54,430 --> 00:00:58,430
with a turning basin of 1,000 meters diameter,

9
00:00:58,430 --> 00:01:02,430
having a depth of at least 20 meters at its shallowest point.

10
00:01:02,430 --> 00:01:04,430
The procedure?

11
00:01:05,430 --> 00:01:11,430
Bury an inline series of four equally spaced 100 kiloton shots

12
00:01:11,430 --> 00:01:17,430
at a depth of 30 meters and a terminal shot of one megaton 50 meters deep,

13
00:01:18,430 --> 00:01:20,430
all shots of minimal fission yield.

14
00:01:21,430 --> 00:01:27,430
Dependent on the nature of the surrounding medium, surround the devices with sufficient borated compounds

15
00:01:27,430 --> 00:01:31,430
to reduce neutron activation by several orders of magnitude.

16
00:01:33,430 --> 00:01:34,430
Detonate.

17
00:01:40,430 --> 00:01:44,430
What is the effect upon radiological safety in the area?

18
00:01:46,430 --> 00:01:49,430
Using energy releases of the order of a megaton

19
00:01:49,430 --> 00:01:52,430
buried at a depth of 50 meters.

20
00:01:53,430 --> 00:02:03,430
With the fireball contained, there would be minimal thermal effects above ground, and

21
00:02:03,430 --> 00:02:08,430
burial of the energy source would confine the radioactivity to a smaller area than a

22
00:02:08,430 --> 00:02:12,430
surface burst.

23
00:02:12,430 --> 00:02:20,430
Over pressures and seismic effects would be acceptably small beyond a radius of 30 kilometers.

24
00:02:20,430 --> 00:02:25,430
in the region of the crater would be washed out into the ocean rapidly and essentially

25
00:02:25,430 --> 00:02:28,430
removed from the biosphere.

26
00:02:28,430 --> 00:02:33,430
The activity on land near the crater would rapidly decay.

27
00:02:33,430 --> 00:02:39,430
And in the matter of economics, the volume excavated would be about 100 million cubic

28
00:02:39,430 --> 00:02:41,430
meters.

29
00:02:41,430 --> 00:02:46,430
The estimated cost of excavation with nuclear energy releases would be significantly less

30
00:02:46,430 --> 00:02:52,430
the estimated 60 million dollars that it would cost for excavation and removal by conventional means

31
00:02:54,430 --> 00:02:59,430
it is interesting to note that the cost of the explosives in the nuclear energy release

32
00:02:59,430 --> 00:03:03,430
would be but a small fraction of the total expenditure

33
00:03:05,430 --> 00:03:09,430
in remote regions where large yields can be safely used

34
00:03:09,430 --> 00:03:15,430
and where economic factors are favorable, such explosions are demonstrably feasible.

35
00:03:17,430 --> 00:03:23,430
With more experience, and the extension of low-cost reduced fission design to smaller

36
00:03:23,430 --> 00:03:28,430
energy releases, the method may one day be applied in more highly populated areas.

37
00:03:30,430 --> 00:03:35,430
In the state of Nevada, one of the table-like mountains was the locale of the first

38
00:03:35,430 --> 00:03:38,430
completely contained underground nuclear explosion.

39
00:03:39,430 --> 00:03:45,430
The 1,700-ton shot was fired on September 19, 1957.

40
00:03:46,430 --> 00:03:49,430
Watch for the shock waves above the arrows.

41
00:03:56,430 --> 00:04:02,430
Rocks dislodged from the mesa's rim left dust trails that formed into a cloud above it.

42
00:04:02,430 --> 00:04:09,430
The ground shock was so slight as to be almost imperceptible to observers four kilometers away.

43
00:04:10,430 --> 00:04:16,430
The peak acceleration at this point amounted to .02 G's.

44
00:04:17,430 --> 00:04:23,430
Actually, only one-half of one percent of total energy released was found to be in the seismic signal,

45
00:04:24,430 --> 00:04:26,430
and no significant overpressures existed.

46
00:04:26,430 --> 00:04:34,430
subsequent construction of an exploration tunnel to zero point permitted close study

47
00:04:34,430 --> 00:04:39,430
of the explosions effects and provided data on the problems of mining in comparable regions

48
00:04:39,430 --> 00:04:47,430
a graphic illustration of the site with a section removed permits visualization of the

49
00:04:47,430 --> 00:04:55,430
physical setup at zero point the tunnel greatly enlarged for the purpose of illustration was

50
00:04:55,430 --> 00:05:01,430
designed to be self-sealing to prevent the ejection of radioactive material.

51
00:05:01,430 --> 00:05:06,430
The initial rectangular room which housed the nuclear device was approximately two

52
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:12,430
meters on a side. The shock from the explosion in the room collapsed the

53
00:05:12,430 --> 00:05:16,430
tunnel a distance of 61 meters from the point of detonation, before the shock

54
00:05:16,430 --> 00:05:22,430
could progress down the tunnel itself. A profile view of the site, a formation of

55
00:05:22,430 --> 00:05:27,430
porous silicate rock known as Oak Springs tough which overlays a dense

56
00:05:27,430 --> 00:05:35,430
crystalline limestone permits relation of the location of zero point 610 meters

57
00:05:35,430 --> 00:05:42,430
from the tunnel entrance 244 meters from the nearest point on the slope of the

58
00:05:42,430 --> 00:05:49,430
Mesa and 274 meters from the Mesa's top

59
00:05:52,430 --> 00:06:05,430
When the shot was fired, the walls of the initial rectangular room were expanded by a pressure of about 6 megabars to a sphere with a 17-meter radius.

60
00:06:07,430 --> 00:06:17,430
The sphere was lined by about 10 centimeters of melted rock weighing 700 tons, which reached a temperature between 1200 and 1500 degrees centigrade.

61
00:06:17,430 --> 00:06:27,430
the shock continuing outward crushed the rock to sand for an additional 20 meters because the

62
00:06:27,430 --> 00:06:35,430
formation contained about 15% water the melted rock cooled rapidly to form glass which contained

63
00:06:35,430 --> 00:06:43,430
the radioactivity when the roof of the glass sphere gave way a collapse proceeded progressively

64
00:06:43,430 --> 00:06:50,430
upward and stopped only after rising about 120 meters above zero point in the

65
00:06:50,430 --> 00:06:55,430
matter of mining in comparable regions it is noted that the shot produced about

66
00:06:55,430 --> 00:07:02,430
400,000 tons of sand the collapse of the initial cavity resulted in more than

67
00:07:02,430 --> 00:07:10,430
200,000 tons of permeable rock and that of all this material only 700 tons

68
00:07:10,430 --> 00:07:13,430
contained practically all the radioactivity.

69
00:07:13,430 --> 00:07:17,430
And this in a well-defined, localized zone.

70
00:07:19,430 --> 00:07:22,430
These facts immediately suggest a whole series

71
00:07:22,430 --> 00:07:24,430
of possible applications in diverse areas.

72
00:07:25,430 --> 00:07:28,430
For example, firing in a dry medium

73
00:07:28,430 --> 00:07:31,430
could result in a high-temperature region

74
00:07:31,430 --> 00:07:33,430
that would persist for an extended period.

75
00:07:34,430 --> 00:07:42,430
And a controlled introduction of water would produce steam for use in generating power or for other industrial purposes.

76
00:07:43,430 --> 00:07:50,430
The use of explosions deep underground beneath large low-grade ore bodies such as copper

77
00:07:50,430 --> 00:07:53,430
immediately suggests more economic recovery

78
00:07:53,430 --> 00:07:57,430
as the explosions would break and loosen large volumes of the ore

79
00:07:57,430 --> 00:07:59,430
to permit leaching of the mineral.

80
00:08:01,430 --> 00:08:05,430
The possibility of releasing petroleum from oil shale

81
00:08:05,430 --> 00:08:08,430
by detonating a device beneath the shale layer

82
00:08:08,430 --> 00:08:11,430
is suggested by the fact that in the shot at the Nevada

83
00:08:11,430 --> 00:08:14,430
Mountain, most of the energy was released as

84
00:08:14,430 --> 00:08:17,430
heat within a radius of about 30 meters of the

85
00:08:17,430 --> 00:08:20,430
center of detonation, and the volume of broken

86
00:08:20,430 --> 00:08:21,430
rock was considerable.

87
00:08:23,430 --> 00:08:26,430
The crushing and heating would distill petroleum

88
00:08:26,430 --> 00:08:28,430
through pipes to the surface.

89
00:08:30,430 --> 00:08:32,430
In the area of public service,

90
00:08:32,430 --> 00:08:39,430
It is suggested that explosions in dry river valleys in desert areas would provide large

91
00:08:39,430 --> 00:08:44,430
underground reservoirs which would serve for flood control and for recharging underground

92
00:08:44,430 --> 00:08:50,430
water supply systems.

93
00:08:50,430 --> 00:08:58,430
Of course, there are problems to be solved and experiments are underway to solve them.

94
00:08:58,430 --> 00:09:05,430
In sum, nuclear explosives will be used within industry for the good of man exactly as chemical

95
00:09:05,430 --> 00:09:07,430
explosives are used.

96
00:09:07,430 --> 00:09:13,430
But in addition, they will be used with an enormously greater effect at a substantially

97
00:09:13,430 --> 00:09:20,430
lesser cost and for many industrial applications which require amounts of packaged energy heretofore

98
00:09:20,430 --> 00:09:27,430
